> I tweaked my keyboard a bit in the last week so any typos are not my fault

# Stuff I Did
## Ran
* Continuing with the trend from last month, I've been pretty satisfied with my general
pace for most of my runs, especially this early in my season. I can sit pretty
comfortably around a ~5:30/km pace on long runs, which is much faster than I expected. I'm
feeling really good about my training even though I'm still not doing full weeks quite
yet, but I did at least up my distance:
```
❯ thoth summary --since month
=== THIS MONTH ===
distance (km): 100.159
average speed (km/hr): 11.927
total_time (hr): 8.81
```

## Played
I had a bit more time for games this month, which was great.
* I played through and wrote a [Rambling Review of Cairn](rr-cairn), which I thoroughly
enjoyed. It was a great "couch game" especially, though I do want to play through it again
on something a bit more powerful than my steam deck since it did start to struggle
performance wise especially towards the end.
* I plaed a ton of [Far Far West](https://store.steampowered.com/app/3124540/Far_Far_West/)
with friends this month as well. It's a blast, they've distilled the exctraction shooter
genre down to its purest form and then also made you cowboys.
* [MOUSE: PI For Hire](https://store.steampowered.com/app/2416450/MOUSE_PI_For_Hire/) was
one I'd had my eye on since it was announced, purely because of the art style. I finished
it just at the end of the month, and though I enjoyed my time with it, I think it is
definitely carried by the visual style. Otherwise it's a serviceable if mediocre boomer
shooter that at least had the foresight not to take itself too seriously and lean into the
cheesy pop-noir aesthetic (pun intended). It probably won't get a full Rambling Review,
but I do recommend it (on sale).

## Read
### Books
* I started this month with *[La Belle Sauvage](https://hardcover.app/books/la-belle-sauvage-the-book-of-dust-volume-one-2017)*,
the first book in Philip Pullman's new Book of Dust trilogy. The original His Dark
Materials books are some of my favorites from my childhood, and I enjoyed this one as
well, though not quite as much. I picked up the second book to read though, so we'll see
how it evolves, especially with the very large time jump I know is coming.
* I had of course ordered *[A Parade of Horribles](https://hardcover.app/books/a-parade-of-horribles-dungeon-crawler-carl-book-8-2026)*
months ago, knowing that it would be a blast. Dungeon Crawler Carl has quickly become my
favorite "pure junk food" series (I mean that as a compliment). Book 8 was no different,
and even though there's more and more political/social commentary as the series goes on,
it's still just as enjoyable. *A Parade of Horribles* sits about in the middle for me -
not my favorite in the series (I think the overall narrative is starting to get a bit
unweildly), but still a blast through and through.
* As a sort of palette cleanser, I picked up [The Design of Everyday Things](https://hardcover.app/books/the-design-of-everyday-things)
again to end the month after abandoning it a while ago (not because it was bad, it just
wasn't good bedtime reading). I think it has some very good nuggets of wisdom in it, but
it's generally just a bit of slog to get through, at least in terms of the writing. Not
bad writing persay, just boring and precise. Glad I read it, don't need to evangelize it
as I've seen others do.

### Not Books
I said in the [april post](2026-apr) that I wanted to reduce the amount of stuff I read
about LLMs in my field. I mostly managed to do that (with the occasionaly slip), and I
definitely felt much better for it. I still did do a fair chunk of technical reading, but
only 2 things really stuck with me this month:
* [Reflections on a Decade of Coding](https://www.scattered-thoughts.net/writing/reflections-on-a-decade-of-coding)
was a fantastic series of posts containing lots of good nuggets of wisdom. I'm not quite
at a decade myself, but I'm approaching it quickly, and it was good to see a lot of my own
trend of "tempered opinions" reflected by someone I respect a lot as an engineer. A
particular quote I liked, from [Things Unlearned](https://www.scattered-thoughts.net/writing/things-unlearned/):

> If you have a mountain of shit to move, how much time should you spend looking for a
> bigger shovel? There's no obviously correct answer - it must depend on the size of the
> mountain, the availability of large shovels, how quickly you have to move it etc. But
> the answer absolutely cannot be 100% of your time. At some point you have to shovel some
> shit.

* I also (re)-read [Egoless Engineering](https://egoless.engineering/), now with a couple
more years of experience and two additional working environments under my belt, and it
resonated a lot more this time. Not that I had missed the point the first time, but moreso
that I hadn't really understood the nuance of a lot of things (I especially used to be
very much in the "I am the engineer, I know better, stay in your lane" camp). I've come
around much more to the "domain experts, not domain owners" approach after working at an
options trading company for a while, where quant traders in particular knew how to write
code, but not how to engineer software. That didn't prevent them from *learning* though
(and that went both ways, I learned a *ton* about the mathematics and optimization behind
pushing literal terabytes of data per hour through these systems), and I found that once I
had taken a step back from the "you do the math, I write the code" school of thought and
into the "we both write the code and I give you advice and feedback on the stuff I'm good
at and vice versa from you to me" way of doing things, our work was much more solid.

## Listened
